The good squares are $1, \dots, L_1-1$, $R_1+1, \dots, L_2-1$, $\dots$, $R_M+1, \dots, N$.
Note that $L_i > 1$ and $R_i < N$, so square 1 and square $N$ are always good.

Since $B$ is small, from any good square $x$, we can reach a range of squares $[x+A, x+B]$.
If we are at a good square $x$, we can reach any good square $y$ in that range.
If the range $[x+A, x+B]$ contains a bad interval, we might be blocked from reaching some squares beyond that interval, or we might be able to jump over it.
